description:
FILED s537a (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-11), found probing #4325.  PRE-EXISTING (main 4a39a1c7 and the s537a tree identical).  A SILENT WRONG: the value flows on as a plain non-reference.

    { package T3; sub TIESCALAR { my $v = $_[1]; bless \$v, $_[0] } sub FETCH { ${$_[0]} } }
    my $u = [];
    tie our $tr, 'T3', $u;
    my $v = eval { $tr }; print "1 ", ref($v), "\n";
    bless $u, 'Q';
    my $w = eval { $tr }; print "2 ", ref($w), "\n";
    sub f { $tr } my $x = f(); print "3 ", ref($x), "\n";

      perl 5.40.3:  1 ARRAY / 2 Q / 3 Q
      PCL:          1  / 2  / 3          (empty: not a reference)

MECHANISM (read, not fixed): %p-leavesub-scalar (cl/pcl-runtime.lisp, the leave rule every sub / eval frame applies to the value it exits with) has no tie arm: a tied box has no class / is-ref flag and its value slot holds the PROXY, so it falls to the final arm `(unbox v)`, which FETCHes and UNBOXES the method's result -- for a reference that drops the reference container and hands the referent's raw vector out as the scalar.  The fix: a tie arm that FETCHes once and applies the SCALAR rule to the result the way %p-assign-snapshot / %p-array-store-scalar read a tied source (a reference result keeps a fresh container with the same referent and class; mind the comment there that the method's result BOX can be the tie object's own referent and carry ITS class -- copy the container flags of the FETCHed VALUE, not the tie object's).  Probe: scratch/s537a/p/m5g.pl in the s537a worktree.  Bar: the reproducer = perl, plus a plain tied string and a tied number returned the same way, plus `for (f()) { $_ = 1 }` not writing through.
